</head>
<%- include('./partials/google') %>

<body>
  <%- include('./partials/nav') %>
  <div class="container-fluid p-3 info-container">
    <div class="intro text-center">
      <h1>What Is Far Reach Co?</h1>
      <hr>
    </div>

    <div class="quick-links">
      <a href="#overview">Overview</a>
      <a href="#who-is-it-for">Who Is It For?</a>
      <a href="#problems-solved">Problems Solved</a>
      <a href="#two-ways-to-play">Two Ways to Play</a>
      <a href="#core-features">Core Features</a>
      <a href="#radio">Far Reach Radio</a>
      <a href="#latest-updates">Latest Updates</a>
      <a href="#comparison">Comparison</a>
      <a href="#get-started">Get Started</a>
    </div>

    <section id="overview">
      <h2>Virtual Tabletop + Persistent Campaigns</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Far Reach Co.</strong> is a <strong>virtual tabletop and campaign management platform</strong>
        built for DMs who want to spend less time wrestling with software and more time telling stories.
        Start using the platform immediately with no upfront cost. Optional subscriptions unlock additional storage
        plus advanced features such as shared Library Packs, Wyrld banners, and Public Wyrld listing controls.
      </p>
      <p>
        Share a link, and your players are in. It's that simple.
      </p>
      <figure class="toolshot-card toolshot-card--focus">
        <img
          src="/assets/screen_shots/vtt-screenshot.png"
          alt="Far Reach Co virtual tabletop showing an active map, tokens, and sidebar controls."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Built for live play: map grid, drag-and-drop tokens, chat/dice, and instant asset access in one workspace.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="who-is-it-for">
      <h2>Who Is This GM-Focused Virtual Tabletop For?</h2>
      <p>
        Far Reach Co. is designed for <strong>Dungeon Masters and Game Masters</strong> who value:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Simplicity</strong> - Intuitive tools that don't require a manual to understand</li>
        <li><strong>Speed</strong> - A responsive interface that keeps pace with your imagination</li>
        <li><strong>Immersion</strong> - A clean experience that keeps players focused on the game, not the platform</li>
        <li><strong>Flexibility</strong> - Freedom to use your own assets without restrictions</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Whether you're a <strong>new DM</strong> running your first session or an <strong>experienced Game Master</strong>
        tired of overengineered platforms, Far Reach Co. gets out of your way and lets you run your game.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="problems-solved">
      <h2>Why GMs Switch: Common Virtual Tabletop Problems Solved</h2>

      <h3>Virtual Tabletop Without Player Accounts</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Solution:</strong> For quick games and one-shots, players join with a single click using a share link.
        No account required. No signup forms. No email verification. Just instant access to the table.
      </p>

      <h3>Start Playing D&amp;D Online in Minutes</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Solution:</strong> Create a table, upload a map, drop in some tokens, share the link. You're playing.
        Far Reach Co. is designed for minimal setup so you can get to the actual game faster.
      </p>

      <h3>Use Your Own Maps and Tokens Without Marketplace Limits</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Solution:</strong> Upload any image you own. Your commissioned character art, your hand-drawn maps,
        your purchased asset packs - if you have the rights to use it, you can upload it. No marketplace gatekeeping,
        no format restrictions.
      </p>

      <h3>Lightweight VTT Interface That Stays Focused</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Solution:</strong> Far Reach Co. prioritizes a clean, focused interface. Features are accessible when
        you need them and invisible when you don't. Your players see the game, not a wall of toolbars.
      </p>

      <h3>Fast Virtual Tabletop Performance on Slower Internet</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Solution:</strong> Far Reach Co. is built for fast load times and responsive performance, even on
        slower connections. We keep things lightweight so players aren't left waiting while the rest of the
        group moves on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="two-ways-to-play">
      <h2>Run One-Shots Fast or Build Persistent Campaigns</h2>
      <p>
        Far Reach Co. offers two approaches depending on your needs:
      </p>

      <h3>Instant-Join Tables for One-Shots and Pickup Games</h3>
      <p>
        Create personal tables directly from your dashboard. These are ideal for:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One-shots</strong> and pickup games</li>
        <li><strong>New groups</strong> trying out the platform</li>
        <li><strong>Casual sessions</strong> where you just want to play</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>How it works:</strong> Create a table, copy the share link, send it to your players. Anyone with the
        link can join instantly - <strong>no account required</strong>. Perfect for getting a game going in minutes.
      </p>

      <h3>Persistent Campaign Workspace for Ongoing Groups</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Wyrlds</strong> are persistent, private campaign environments for groups that play together regularly.
        Within a Wyrld:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Players link their character sheets</strong> to the campaign for easy access</li>
        <li><strong>Resources are shared</strong> - lore records, images, calendars, and tables belong to the Wyrld</li>
        <li><strong>Permissions are controlled</strong> - set who can edit, who can view, and who can manage</li>
        <li><strong>Community discussion stays in-context</strong> - run thread-based planning and session discussion inside the Wyrld</li>
        <li><strong>Public discovery can be configured</strong> - list your Wyrld, set join mode/capacity, and feature one onboarding record</li>
        <li><strong>Everything stays organized</strong> - campaign content is encapsulated in one place</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>How it works:</strong> Create a Wyrld, generate an invite link, and share it with your players. Players
        create free accounts to join, giving them persistent access to their linked character sheets and campaign resources
        across sessions. The GM can promote trusted players to Managers who can create and edit Wyrld content.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your personal dashboard remains separate - a private workspace for your own tables and records that don't belong
        to any campaign.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="core-features">
      <h2>Core Features for GM-Focused Online Campaigns</h2>
      <div class="toolshot-grid toolshot-grid--three">
        <figure class="toolshot-card">
          <img
            src="/assets/screen_shots/library_screenshot.png"
            alt="Library app interface with folders, search, and asset cards."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Library scope + folder system for fast session prep.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="toolshot-card">
          <img
            src="/assets/screen_shots/character_sheet_adren.png"
            alt="D&D 5E character sheet with stats and attacks."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Live character sheets linked to campaign play.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="toolshot-card toolshot-card--smallshot">
          <img
            src="/assets/screen_shots/strahd_library_pack.png"
            alt="Library pack management panel with metadata and image list."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Pack publishing and discover workflows for shared assets.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <h3>Invite-Link Virtual Tabletop (No Signup for Players)</h3>
      <p>
        A <strong>platform-agnostic virtual tabletop</strong> with real-time collaboration. Use it for D&D, Pathfinder,
        Call of Cthulhu, or any tabletop RPG. Features include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Drag-and-drop tokens and map images</li>
        <li>Drawing tools for quick annotations</li>
        <li>Real-time sync - everyone sees changes instantly</li>
        <li>Clean interface that doesn't overwhelm your players</li>
      </ul>


      <h3>D&amp;D 5E Character Sheets Linked to Campaigns</h3>
      <p>
        Purpose-built character sheets with a fresh, intuitive design. Create sheets from your dashboard and optionally
        link them to Wyrlds for campaign play. Designed to feel familiar if you've used paper sheets.
      </p>

      <h3>Campaign Records and Worldbuilding Notes</h3>
      <p>
        Create lore entries, session notes, custom monster stat blocks, and other campaign resources. Personal records
        stay in your dashboard; Wyrld records are shared with your campaign group.
      </p>

      <h3>Asset Freedom: Upload Your Own Maps and Tokens</h3>
      <p>
        Upload and organize your own tokens, maps, and images. No restrictions on what you can upload - if it's your
        image, you can use it.
      </p>

      <h3>Shared Library Packs (Pro)</h3>
      <p>
        Publish and discover curated image packs for faster prep. Library Packs are a paid feature:
        <strong>Pro User</strong> unlocks personal-scope packs and <strong>Pro Wyrld</strong> unlocks Wyrld-scope packs.
      </p>

      <h3>Tabletop Audio Streaming With Far Reach Radio</h3>
      <p>
        Far Reach Radio gives GMs a browser-based way to stream session music and ambience in sync. Create a channel,
        pick a playlist, share one listener link, and your group hears the same audio mood together.
      </p>

      <h3>Public Wyrlds + Featured Records</h3>
      <p>
        List a Wyrld in the public directory to help players discover campaigns. Owners can configure invite-only
        or request-based joining, optional capacity limits, and a featured record that acts as a campaign landing page.
      </p>

      <h3>Community Discussion Boards</h3>
      <p>
        Each Wyrld includes a built-in discussion board with threads and replies so campaign planning, announcements,
        and table talk stay attached to the campaign itself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="radio">
      <h2>Far Reach Radio: Synced Audio for Online Sessions</h2>
      <p>
        Most groups stitch together Discord bots, playlists, and manual sync steps. Far Reach Radio removes that setup
        friction with a lightweight tabletop audio streaming workflow built for play sessions.
      </p>
      <figure class="toolshot-card toolshot-card--smallshot">
        <img
          src="/assets/screen_shots/radio_host_control.png"
          alt="Far Reach Radio host control panel with listener link and playlist controls."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Host panel with one-click listener sharing and fast mood switching during live scenes.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Host-controlled broadcast</strong> - GMs switch playlist mood in real time</li>
        <li><strong>Listener link access</strong> - players join audio quickly with no account</li>
        <li><strong>Browser-first delivery</strong> - no app install or audio bot configuration</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Learn more on the <a href="/radio">Far Reach Radio page</a> and launch at
        <a href="https://radio.farreachco.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">radio.farreachco.com</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="comparison">
      <h2>Far Reach Co vs Traditional VTT Platforms</h2>
      <p>
        Far Reach Co. isn't trying to be the most feature-packed VTT on the market. Instead, it focuses on:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Lower barrier to entry</strong> - Players can join without accounts for casual games</li>
        <li><strong>Faster setup</strong> - Less configuration, more playing</li>
        <li><strong>Unrestricted assets</strong> - Use your own images without marketplace limitations</li>
        <li><strong>Cleaner interface</strong> - Fewer buttons, less clutter, more immersion</li>
        <li><strong>Free-first approach</strong> - core play tools stay broadly accessible, while Pro focuses on campaign scale, presentation, and discovery</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you want a platform that does one thing well - <strong>getting you and your players into a game quickly</strong> -
        Far Reach Co. is built for you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="who-built-it">
      <h2>Who Built It?</h2>
      <p>
        Far Reach Co. was created by a small group of friends - <strong>musicians, artists, developers, and teachers</strong> -
        who needed a way to keep playing D&D despite living across the country. The tools we built for ourselves became
        the platform you're reading about now.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/about-us">Read our full story →</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="get-started">
      <h2>Get Started</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Want to try it now?</strong> <a href="/vtt/guest/start">Start a no-signup VTT sandbox</a> or
        <a href="/register">create a free account</a> to access your dashboard and build tables in minutes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Need a fast onboarding path?</strong> Follow the <a href="/vtt-guide">virtual tabletop setup guide</a>
        and launch your first table quickly.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Just exploring?</strong> Check out our <a href="/resources/free-assets">free resources</a> including starter
        token packs, the complete D&D 5E SRD, and tutorial videos.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Looking for a campaign?</strong> Browse <a href="/wyrlds/public">Public Wyrlds</a> and review featured onboarding records.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Have questions?</strong> Join our <a href="https://discord.gg/Yzw3xZysxe">Discord community</a> -
        we're happy to help.
      </p>
    </section>

  </div>
  <%- include('./partials/footer') %>
</body>

</html>
